<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sergius IV of Naples</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1036</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sergius IV was duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> from about 1002 to around 1036. Driven from his city in 1027 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandulf-iv-of-capua">Pandulf IV of Capua</a>, he recovered it in 1029 with the help of Norman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, and in 1030 granted their leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rainulf-drengot">Rainulf Drengot</a> the town of Aversa, the first Norman lordship in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. He later retired to a monastery.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sergius-iv-of-naples.jpg" alt="Sergius IV of Naples" width="320" height="460" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MapMaster · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Italy_1000_AD.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Sergius succeeded his father, John IV, and like him was nominally a Byzantine vassal. In 1024, when Pilgrim, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, was besieging Capua on behalf of Emperor Henry II, Sergius submitted to him, although his own duchy was not threatened. By this he acquired a reputation for weakness in the eyes of Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandulf-iv-of-capua">Pandulf IV of Capua</a>, whom Pilgrim had defeated.</p><p>In 1026 Pandulf, returned from captivity, besieged his old capital, then ruled by Pandulf V, the count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teanum-sidicinum">Teano</a>. Basil Boioannes, the Greek catapan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, negotiated a surrender and gave Pandulf V safe conduct to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, where Sergius offered him asylum and so earned Pandulf IV's enmity. In 1027, after Boioannes had been recalled, Pandulf IV attacked Naples and quickly captured it, by treachery according to some. Pandulf V fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and Sergius went into hiding.</p><p>Fortune turned in 1029, when the Norman Rainulf Drengot abandoned Pandulf. Sergius and his nephew, Duke John V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaeta">Gaeta</a>, sent an embassy asking for Rainulf's help, and with it Pandulf was chased from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and Sergius reinstated. Early in 1030 Sergius gave Rainulf the county of Aversa as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>, the first Norman principality in the region, and his own sister in marriage. These grants made Sergius one of the prime catalysts in the growth of Norman power in the Mezzogiorno.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1034 Pandulf IV instigated a revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorrento">Sorrento</a> and annexed it to Capua. In the same year Sergius' sister died and Rainulf returned to Pandulf's side. Broken in spirit, Sergius retired to the monastery of the Holy Saviour in insula maris, and was succeeded by his son, John V.</p><p>When John went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> to seek aid from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, Sergius came out of retirement for a time to act as regent, and on John's return he went back to his monastery. He was still living there as late as June 1036 and probably died shortly afterwards. His two retirements explain why Amatus of Montecassino refers to him twice becoming a monk.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sergius IV of Naples” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1030 Duke <b>Sergius IV</b> of the Duchy of Naples rewarded the Norman mercenary leader Rainulf Drengot with the town and district of Aversa, between Naples and Capua, to guard his frontier against the Principality of Capua.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-aversa">County of Aversa</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.84,14.25&amp;year=1026" aria-label="Naples on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4899 706 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Sergius IV of Naples"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5349 976h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5349 976h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=40.84,14.25&amp;year=1026">What was here in 1026? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
